## Prerequisites

- `@/types/index.js` must export `ParseBrainAideResult.ok` with the field
  `studyPlaybook: string` (camelCase). **Already in place** — the parser cycle
  landed first; verify by reading `src/types/index.ts` lines 585-589 (the
  `ParseBrainAideResult` union literal).
- `@/service/parseBrainAide/index.js` must export `parseBrainAide`,
  `parseBrainAideFromString`, and `interpolateArgs`, and the parser must
  recognize the four marker pairs in the required order
  prose → playbook → studyPlaybook → research, returning ok results that carry
  all four typed body fields. **Already in place** — verify by reading
  `MARKER_PAIRS` (lines 17-22) and `parseBrainAideFromString`'s return shape
  (line 399) in `src/service/parseBrainAide/index.ts`.

If either prerequisite is not in place, this plan cannot compile and must
not start.

## Plan

### [x] 1. Update obsidianBrainAideTemplate to emit the four-section body in the required order

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`, `.aide/docs/agent-readable-code.md`, `.aide/docs/progressive-disclosure.md`

The current template emits a heading-bounded body (`## Prose`, `## Playbook hub`, `## Research hub`) which the marker-bounded parser rejects. This step replaces the body composition entirely so the output is four marker-bounded sections in the required order, with the playbook section trimmed and the new studyPlaybook section sourced from the multi-month-tested skill prose. The frontmatter and platform branching at the top of the file are unchanged.

- [x] 1a. Replace the body half of `obsidianBrainAideTemplate/index.ts` (everything after the `---\n` close fence in the returned string) with four marker-bounded sections in this exact order:
  - `<!-- aide-prose-start --> ... <!-- aide-prose-end -->` carrying the existing prose bytes (the `Your brain is...` paragraph block, currently between `## Prose` and `## Playbook hub`). Keep the existing storage-agnostic-but-`mcp__brain__*`-flavored vocabulary — the prose section's existing bytes already pass the storage-agnostic prose tests. Add a sentence describing the new study-playbook entry-point at `coding-playbook/study-playbook.md` to the prose so it lists all THREE entry-point artifacts (the existing prose mentions only two).
  - `<!-- aide-playbook-start --> ... <!-- aide-playbook-end -->` carrying the playbook section content TRIMMED to pure coding conventions (see step 2 — that is a separate step but its output is what lands in this section here; sequence step 1 first to establish the marker scaffold, then step 2 trims the bytes inside).
  - `<!-- aide-study-playbook-start --> ... <!-- aide-study-playbook-end -->` carrying the multi-month-tested navigation prose. Source: read `.claude/skills/study-playbook/SKILL.md`, drop the YAML frontmatter (lines between the leading `---` fences) and the single-line `# /study-playbook — Load Coding Playbook Context` title plus the one-line "Navigate the coding playbook hub..." preamble that names the skill itself, and write the remaining substantive teaching content verbatim. The remaining content is everything from the `Navigate the coding playbook hub in the brain...` paragraph through `Don't load unrelated sections "just in case."` — the Step 1 / Step 2 / Step 3 process headings and prose, the Navigation Rules block with the depth-counting example, the link-traversal semantics, the never-re-read rule, and the stay-in-scope rule. Replace the H1 with `# Study Playbook` (the bundled artifact's title). All section headings inside this section MUST be `### ` or deeper (not `## `) so the parser walker does not see them as section boundaries — demote any `## ` headings from the source skill content to `### ` here. The `[[wikilink]]` substrings, "hub" word usages, and other connector-flavored vocabulary STAY — the parent intent's user-owned-section-bytes carve-out explicitly permits them inside studyPlaybook bytes.
  - `<!-- aide-research-start --> ... <!-- aide-research-end -->` carrying the existing research bytes (the `# Research` block currently between `## Research hub` and end-of-string). Demote the existing nested headings to `### ` if they are not already (they currently are `### Domains`, `### How to Use This Index`, `### Domain Hubs`, `### Contents` — verify after the edit).
- [x] 1b. Update the function-level JSDoc above `obsidianBrainAideTemplate` to describe the new four-section body. Replace the `three body sections in this order` enumeration with `four body sections in this order` and list the new section between playbook and research. Drop or reword the `Format spec: .aide/docs/brain-aide.md` comment if its bullets reference the old shape — only the section count and ordering paragraph need to change. The platform-branching JSDoc bullet stays. The "Nested headings inside any section MUST use `### ` or deeper" bullet is updated to reference markers rather than `## ` boundaries: under marker grammar, nested `## ` is silently ignored by the parser — but for visual consistency and to match the existing tests' "no nested `## ` lines" assertions, keep the requirement.
- [x] 1c. Verify by running `npx tsc --noEmit` and `npx vitest run src/service/install/provisionBrain/obsidianBrainAideTemplate/`. The template's existing tests should now pass because the body emits valid marker pairs that round-trip through `parseBrainAide`. Tests added in step 4 are not yet in place — running just this step's tests is fine.

### 2. Trim the playbook body section to pure coding conventions

Read: `coding-playbook/foundations/conventions`

The current playbook section in the template includes both the conventions skeleton (Task Routing table, Always Read First, Sections, Contents) AND a `### How to Use This Index` paragraph (lines 70-72 of the current template) that is procedural navigation prose. The amendment moves all procedural navigation prose into the new studyPlaybook section. This step strips the playbook section to pure conventions only.

- [x] Remove the `### How to Use This Index` heading and its paragraph from inside the playbook section's bytes (the paragraph that begins "Read this note first. Each section links to a section hub..."). Keep these inside the playbook section: the `# Coding Playbook` H1, the `### Task Routing` heading and table skeleton, the `### Always Read First` heading and its `[[your-conventions-note]]` / `[[your-folder-structure-note]]` placeholder list, the `### Sections` heading, the `### Contents` heading. Anything that is procedural narration about HOW to read the playbook moves out (it now lives in studyPlaybook); anything that is structural scaffolding for the user's own conventions stays. After the trim the playbook section is a pure structural seed — tables, headings, placeholder wikilinks for the user's domain content. No instructions on how to navigate. **(folded into Step 1's single-pass body replacement per the plan's explicit allowance)**

