Read: `coding-playbook/workflow/testing`

The cascade context from the parser cycle showed 101 failures across 7 files all stemming from the bundled template still being three-section. After step 1 lands, the template emits four sections; after step 6 lands, the orchestrator returns six steps; after step 7 lands, the test fixtures match. This step is the contract that everything else stays green.

- [x] 8a. Run `npx tsc --noEmit` from the project root. Exit code must be 0. Any errors are most likely a missed return-shape update (resolveBrainAideBody) or a stale `results[N]` index that compiles but is wrong (TypeScript will not catch the latter — that's caught in 8b).
- [x] 8b. Run `npx vitest run` from the project root. All tests must pass. Specifically, the previously-failing 101 tests across 7 files must now pass because the template emits four marker-bounded sections that round-trip through the parser. If any test fails:
  - A `parseBrainAide` or `parseBrainAideFromString` test failing → the bytes step 1 wrote into one of the four sections are wrong (likely missing a marker, wrong marker order, or nested markers in the studyPlaybook section because a `## ` heading wasn't demoted).
  - A consumer test (cli/sync, tools/brain, install service) failing → a downstream consumer expects a different return shape than this plan delivered, OR the test fixture in that file uses the old three-section body. In the latter case the fix is a fixture update in that test file; flag it for the orchestrator rather than expanding scope here.
  - A new test added in steps 3 or 7 failing → the added bytes did not satisfy the assertion. Re-read the source skill content for step 1 / re-check the trim for step 2.
- [x] 8c. If 8b reveals consumer-test fixture failures outside this plan's scope (e.g. a tools/brain test that hand-writes a three-section brain.aide), STOP and surface the list of failing files to the orchestrator. Updating those fixtures is a separate plan cycle scoped to those modules. Do NOT silently edit fixtures outside `provisionBrain/`. The exception is fixtures inside `parseBrainAide/index.test.ts` that already test the four-section grammar — those should already be passing because the parser cycle landed first; if they fail after this plan's changes, something in this plan regressed the parser-side contract and step 1 needs revisiting.

## Decisions

**1. Step builder file structure: inline, mirroring existing peers.**
The existing `buildPlaybookStep`, `buildResearchStep`, `buildBrainAideStep`, `buildBrainRootStep`, and `buildBrainMcpStep` are all defined inline in `provisionBrain/index.ts` (verified by reading the file: 469 lines, all five builders inline as private functions above the default export). The progressive-disclosure tier model and the playbook's "extract helpers into subfolders" rule both prefer subfolders, BUT the spec's Decision Point 1 directive is unambiguous: "the new builder must look like its peers." Mirroring the existing inline structure is the right call for THIS plan because (a) the existing structure already exists in place and is consistent within the module, (b) extracting only the new helper into its own subfolder would create asymmetry that confuses future readers, (c) the spec's authoritative scope explicitly says mirror the existing pattern, and (d) extracting all five existing helpers AND the new one into subfolders is a separate refactor that belongs in its own cycle (it would more than double the plan's surface area and risk regressions in already-working code). The new helper goes inline above the default export, between `buildPlaybookStep` and `buildResearchStep` in dependency order.

**2. studyPlaybook section content sourcing rule.** The implementor reads `.claude/skills/study-playbook/SKILL.md` and copies the substantive teaching content verbatim into the new bundled template section. The exact extraction rule: drop the YAML frontmatter (lines between the leading `---` fences); drop the leading `# /study-playbook — Load Coding Playbook Context` H1 title and the one-line `Navigate the coding playbook hub in the brain and load only the sections relevant to the current task.` preamble that names the skill itself; KEEP everything from the next paragraph (`The brain backend is configurable...` — wait, this preamble is also skill-self-referential and mentions calling `aide_brain` as a separate tool, which is the OLD skill shape — drop this one too) through the end of the file. The remaining substantive teaching content starts at `## Step 1: Read the Playbook Hub` and runs through `Don't load unrelated sections "just in case."`. Replace the `## Step N` headings with `### Step N` (demote to H3 so the no-`^## .+$` test still passes). Replace the `## Navigation Rules` heading with `### Navigation Rules` (same reason). Add a `# Study Playbook` H1 at the top of the section (the artifact's own title — the user-owned artifact needs an H1 of its own; the demoted Step headings sit underneath it). The implementor is NOT free to paraphrase, summarize, or "modernize" the prose — every sentence is multi-month-tested and the carve-out's whole point is that the content travels with the bundled template byte-identical. The two exceptions documented above (drop frontmatter, drop the skill-self-referential preambles, demote `##` to `###`, add the artifact H1) are mechanical transformations; everything else is verbatim.

**3. Playbook section trim scope.** What stays in the trimmed playbook section: the H1 (`# Coding Playbook`), the `### Task Routing` heading + table skeleton (`| Task domain | Section |\n|-------------|---------|`), the `### Always Read First` heading + the `[[your-conventions-note]]` and `[[your-folder-structure-note]]` placeholder list, the `### Sections` heading, the `### Contents` heading. What moves out: the `### How to Use This Index` heading and its paragraph (currently lines 70-72 of the template's playbook section in `obsidianBrainAideTemplate/index.ts`). After the trim, the playbook section is pure structural scaffolding for the user's domain content — tables, headings, placeholder wikilinks. Procedural narration about HOW to use the playbook is gone (it now lives in studyPlaybook). The single sentence "These notes are required reading for every task..." that introduces the Always Read First list is borderline — it's instructive but it's also a structural label for the placeholder list. **Keep it** — it explains what the list is, not how to navigate the playbook globally; it's a description of one section's role, which is structural scaffolding.

**4. resolveBrainAideBody return shape.** Field order in the returned literal follows marker order: `{ playbook, studyPlaybook, research }`. The destructure on the parseResult side mirrors the same order. Field order in the returned object is conventional (TypeScript doesn't enforce it), but matching marker order makes the helper's intent self-documenting. The helper stays inline in `provisionBrain/index.ts`; same rationale as Decision 1.

**5. Step ordering for safety: template first, then orchestrator step list, then test updates.** The plan order is: (1) template emits four sections → (2) playbook trimmed → (3) template tests pass → (4) resolveBrainAideBody extends → (5) buildStudyPlaybookStep added → (6) orchestrator wires it in and returns six steps → (7) provisionBrain tests updated → (8) full test suite. This ordering matters because the parser is now strict-failure: as soon as the parser cycle landed, the existing three-section template started returning `malformed-body` from `parseBrainAideFromString`. The 101 failing tests cited in the cascade context will START GREEN as soon as step 1 lands (template emits valid four-section markers). The orchestrator step-list update in step 6 is independent of the template fix in step 1 — they could in principle be reordered — BUT putting the template first means the implementor can verify each step's correctness against an immediately-passing local test suite. Reverse-ordering would leave step 1's correctness unverifiable until step 6 also lands. The chosen order keeps every step independently testable.

**6. Test fixture organization: extend existing fixtures in place, do not introduce new test files.** The provisionBrain test file at `index.test.ts` is the canonical home for orchestrator-level tests. The new study-playbook step's tests go in describe blocks that mirror the existing playbook describe blocks, in the same file, in marker order. The new studyPlaybook section's content tests go in the bundled template's test file. Splitting either set of new tests into its own `*.test.ts` file would fragment the test surface (every implementor would need to grep two files to understand what's tested for each step). The existing test files are large but well-organized; adding to them keeps the testing surface consistent.

**7. JSDoc updates: every JSDoc that enumerates body sections / step counts / artifact counts changes.** The functions whose JSDoc must change:
- `obsidianBrainAideTemplate` — three sections becomes four; describe the new section in position 3.
- `resolveBrainAideBody` — return-shape docstring changes; the paragraph beginning "Returns `{ playbook, research }` on a successful parse..." becomes "Returns `{ playbook, studyPlaybook, research }` on a successful parse...".
- `provisionBrain` — five InitStep items becomes six; insert a new bullet between bullet 3 and the old bullet 4; renumber. The "Two idempotency modes coexist" sentence updates its parentheticals.
- `buildStudyPlaybookStep` — net-new JSDoc, mirrors `buildPlaybookStep`'s JSDoc with the marker-pair name and artifact path adjusted.
JSDocs on `buildBrainAideStep`, `buildBrainRootStep`, `buildPlaybookStep`, `buildResearchStep`, `buildBrainMcpStep`, `resolveBrainAideConfig`, `exists`, `safeReadFile`, `brainRootExists` do NOT change — they describe their own helper, not the orchestrator's step count.

**8. Idempotency consistency: presence-only, mirrors playbook and research.** `buildStudyPlaybookStep` uses the exact same idempotency check as its peers: `if (await exists(filePath)) return { ..., status: "exists" }`. No byte comparison, no drift detection against the bundled template's `studyPlaybook` section bytes. Once the user has the file, the bytes are theirs; even if the bundled template's studyPlaybook section evolves in a future package release, the on-disk study-playbook.md never gets replaced or re-suggested by provisionBrain. This matches the spec's outcome 4 ("playbook, study-playbook, and research entry-point artifacts are treated as one-time seeds") and the parent spec's directory-level seed-semantic invariant for everything provisionBrain touches outside `.mcp.json`.

**9. Artifact path: `coding-playbook/study-playbook.md`, same directory as the playbook artifact.** The path is hardcoded in `buildStudyPlaybookStep` as `join(brainPath, "coding-playbook", "study-playbook.md")` — same `coding-playbook/` directory as `coding-playbook/coding-playbook.md`. The two artifacts live as siblings inside one folder. The decision rationale: (a) the spec's outcomes name this exact path explicitly, (b) the agent-side `study-playbook` skill becomes minimal and points at `coding-playbook/study-playbook.md` (not at the brain root), (c) co-locating with the playbook makes it discoverable when someone navigates the brain root via filesystem, and (d) the path string is the only place inside `buildStudyPlaybookStep` that the storage-specific `.md` filename appears — the function name, parameter names, local variable names, and JSDoc all stay storage-agnostic per the spec's outcome 9 (storage-agnostic vocabulary). The `coding-playbook/` directory already exists on disk (it's pre-created by `BRAIN_ROOT_DIRS` in `buildBrainRootStep`), so no new directory creation is needed.

**10. Storage-agnostic identifiers; carve-outs documented.** Code surfaces (function names, parameter names, JSDoc, log strings, InitStep `name` fields) all stay storage-agnostic. The new builder is `buildStudyPlaybookStep` (camelCase, no `Hub` suffix, no connector prefix). The new InitStep name is `"Study-playbook entry-point"` (matches the playbook's `"Playbook entry-point"` and research's `"Research entry-point"` patterns). The artifact path constant inside `buildStudyPlaybookStep` is the only place in `provisionBrain/index.ts` where the `.md` extension appears; that's allowed under the spec's storage-agnostic rule because the path describes where the bundled-default editor-backed Markdown brain stores its artifact, not a code-level dispatch axis. The studyPlaybook BYTES (which include `[[wikilinks]]`, `hub` words, and other connector vocabulary) live inside `obsidianBrainAideTemplate/index.ts` and travel into the artifact at install time; per the parent intent's user-owned-section-bytes carve-out, those bytes legitimately ship the bundled template's connector-specific navigation prose. The carve-out applies to the section bytes only — not to any identifier or string constant in `provisionBrain/index.ts`.
